(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["cs-easy-2026-003"]={"v":1,"id":"cs-easy-2026-003","title":"Advance Level Test-3 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 CS","sourceTitle":"Advance Level Test-3 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 CS","branch":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING","code":"CS","provider":"DVRUO EASY","providerKey":"easy","series":"GATE 2026 Online Test Series - Computer Science Engineering","seriesId":"cs-easy-2026","num":3,"category":"advance","year":"2026","format":"A","durationSec":10800,"info":{"meta":"Package: GATE 2026 GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Computer Science Engineering • Questions: 65","package":"GATE 2026 GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Computer Science Engineering"},"source":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING/MADE EASY/GATE_2026_GATE_2025_Online_Test_Series_Computer_Science_Engineering/006_Advance Level Test-3 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 CS.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Count the number of squares in the following figure:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5d/5d673861005a2eca5f5b.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"18"},{"key":"B","html":"14"},{"key":"C","html":"10"},{"key":"D","html":"9"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/02/02f0ebec61bb871832b2.png\" /></div>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Find the odd one out from the given sequence:<br />\n1, 5, 14, 30, 50, 55, 91</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"5"},{"key":"B","html":"50"},{"key":"C","html":"55"},{"key":"D","html":"91"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d8/d877bc9077fbb88d7e98.png\" /></div>"},{"n":3,"type":"NAT","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A man standing on the bank of a river observes that the angle subtended by a tree on the opposite bank is 60°. When he retries 36 m away from the bank, he find the angle to be 30°, the breadth of the river is ____ m.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[18,18],"answerText":"18","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>18 (18 - 18)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ea/ea9a13046acff988548e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>In a race of 200 m, <em>A</em> can beat <em>B</em> by 31 m and <em>C</em> by 18 m. In a race of 350 m, <em>C</em> will beat B by</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"22.75 m"},{"key":"B","html":"25 m"},{"key":"C","html":"19.5 m"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/62/62534949b5b66d9b40aa.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b6/b6a9430938bda3b9cb50.png\" /></div>"},{"n":5,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Astronomer: Most stars are born in groups of thousands, each star in a group forming from the same parent cloud of gas. Each cloud has a unique homogeneous chemical composition. Therefore whenever two stars have the same chemical composition as each other, they must have originated from the same cloud of gas.<br />\nWhich of the following, if true, would most strengthen the astronomer’s argument?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"In some groups of stars, not every star originated from the same parent cloud of gas."},{"key":"B","html":"Clouds of gas of similar or identical chemical composition may be remote from each other."},{"key":"C","html":"Whenever a star forms, it inherits the chemical composition of its parent cloud of gas."},{"key":"D","html":"Many stars in vastly different parts of the universe are quite similar in their chemical compositions."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nIt is given that each cloud has a unique homogeneous chemical composition and the stars originated from the same cloud of gas have the same chemical composition. Thus, it can be concluded that whenever a star forms, it inherits the chemical composition of its parent cloud of gas. Hence, option (c) is correct.</p>"},{"n":6,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>People tend to estimate the likelihood of an event’s occurrence according to its salience; that is, according to how strongly and how often it comes to their attention. By placement and headlines, newspapers emphasize stories about local crime over stories about crime elsewhere and about many other major events.<br />\nIt can be concluded on the basis of the statements above that, if they are true, which of the following is most probably also true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The language used in newspaper headlines about local crime is inflammatory and fails to respect the rights of suspects."},{"key":"B","html":"The press is the news medium that focuses people attention most strongly on local crimes."},{"key":"C","html":"Readers of local news in newspapers tend to overestimate the amount of crime in their own localities relative to the amount of crime in other places."},{"key":"D","html":"None of the events concerning other people that are reported in newspapers is so salient in people’s minds as their own personal experiences."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nWe know that newspapers&rsquo; coverage of local crimes is stronger than that of non-local crimes.<br />\nNothing is said about frequency of coverage so, we can not assume anything about it. However, all else being equal, we can assume that people tend to think the local crimes outnumber non-local crimes.</p>"},{"n":7,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>The value of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/5d/5d554f17ca74f8d1bd33.png\" /> is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2"},{"key":"B","html":"7"},{"key":"C","html":"8"},{"key":"D","html":"33"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/aa/aaa4940bdd8b88c1c966.png\" /></div>"},{"n":8,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The difference between the compound interest and the simple interest earned on a sum of money at the end of 4 years is <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/46/465b74f21b5aecea6328.png\" /> To find out the sum, which of the following information given in the statements <em>P</em> and <em>Q</em> is/are necessary?<br />\nP : Amount of simple interest accrued after 4 years.<br />\nQ : Rate of interest per annum.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only P is necessary"},{"key":"B","html":"Only Q is necessary"},{"key":"C","html":"Either P or Q is necessary"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither P nor Q is necessary"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nTo find the sum, difference between C.I. and S.I., the time and the rate of interest are needed.<br />\n&there4; Only Q is necessary.</p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A boat takes 19 hours for travelling downstream from point A to point B and coming back to a point C midway between A and B. If the velocity of the stream is 4 kmph and the speed of the boat in still water is 14 kmph, what is the distance between A and B?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"160 km"},{"key":"B","html":"180 km"},{"key":"C","html":"200 km"},{"key":"D","html":"220 km"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/35/35bf24fa038429ae0e8b.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":10,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Given below are 4 pairs of words. In three of the pairs, the words are having a meaning similar to each other whereas in one of the pairs, it is the opposite. Identify the option where the words do not have similar meaning.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"CONTRAPTION - SIMPLE"},{"key":"B","html":"JOCUND - MERRY"},{"key":"C","html":"INSCRUTABLE - ENIGMATIC"},{"key":"D","html":"LOUCHE - INDECENT"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nContraption means a complex arrangement.</p>"},{"n":11,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the non-deterministic finite automation (NFA) shown in the figure:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/81/81a2b49060f605e7b6f9.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Start <em>x</em> is the starting state of the automation. Let the language accepted by the NFA with y as the only accepting state be <em>L</em><sub>1</sub>. Similarly the language accepted by the NFA with z as the only accepting state be <em>L</em><sub>2</sub>. Which of the following statement about <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> is true?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>L</em><sub>1</sub> = <em>L</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>L</em><sub>2</sub> ⊂ <em>L</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>L</em><sub>1</sub> ⊂ <em>L</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7e/7e3f6b2f2dc826be7aaa.png\" /></div>"},{"n":12,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>The eigen values of the matrix <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/32/326e8fb67e1d59631474.png\" /> are in ratio 1 : 1 : 4. If ‘a’ is a positive real number, then the value of ‘<em>a</em>’ will be ________. </p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[6,6],"answerText":"6","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>6</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/06/06ae244e5aace2432faf.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":13,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following regular expressions over alphabet {a, b}, where the notation (a + b)+ means</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/53/53a876c7c67f78f2fca1.png\" /></div>\n\n<p><br />\nLet <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> be the languages defined by <em>r</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>r</em><sub>2</sub>, respectively. Which of the following regular expressions define <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> ∩ <em>L</em><sub>2</sub>?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/d5/d545401b60e22741b59e.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/16/167f0d80d51887531634.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/93/935c978af7c28bd7d3d2.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/18/189daf0cc81b44ef235a.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nThe easiest way here is to check the minimum length string that is accepted by both <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> &cap; <em>L</em><sub>2</sub>, in this<br />\ncase it is &ldquo;ba&rdquo;, the only option which matches with that is (c).</p>"},{"n":14,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>A fair coin is tossed to determine from which bag a ball will be drawn.<br />\nIf the coin shows Heads, a ball is picked from Bag 1, which contains 6 red and 9 blue balls.<br />\nIf the coin shows Tails, a ball is picked from Bag 2, which contains 5 red and 5 blue balls.<br />\nAfter the draw, the ball obtained is observed to be blue.<br />\nFind the probability that the ball was drawn from Bag __________. (Upto 2 decimal places)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.54,0.54],"answerText":"0.54","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.54</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/79/79c91453cea99418f356.png\" /></div>"},{"n":15,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Which of following are true about TCP?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"TCP is a connectionless protocol."},{"key":"B","html":"TCP uses sequence numbers to ensure in order delivery."},{"key":"C","html":"TCP does not perform flow control."},{"key":"D","html":"TCP uses 3-way handshake to establish connection."}],"answer":["B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, d)</p>"},{"n":16,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\nS<sub>1</sub> : Direct access to a record can be supported efficiently if the record is stored in contiguous memory blocks.<br />\nS<sub>2</sub> : In contiguous allocation, random access is possible in constant time because the address of any record can be computed directly from its position.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only S<sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only S<sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5d/5da1d2d66a4022459646.png\" /></div>"},{"n":17,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A processor overflow flag is represent as OV(<em>a</em>, <em>b</em>, <em>c</em>) where arguments a is MSB of operand 1, b is MSB of operand 2 and c is the MSB of the result after computing a and b. The OV(<em>a</em>, <em>b</em>, <em>c</em>) of PSW becomes 1 after</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>a</em>′<em>b</em>′<em>c</em> + <em>a</em>′<em>bc</em>′"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>a</em>′<em>b</em>′<em>c</em> + <em>abc</em>′"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>ab</em>′<em>c</em>′ + <em>ab</em>′<em>c</em>"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>a</em>′<em>b</em>′<em>c</em> + <em>abc</em>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/07/07e6a3740a1054f474d2.png\" /></div>"},{"n":18,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider PLA circuit shown in the figure below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d9/d9584a5c6ec7e8a3dc25.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The output <em>f</em> (<em>A</em>, <em>B</em>, <em>C</em>) is equal to</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/aa/aa6cbaed6fa147266922.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e0/e00ed2568c0e3b97e284.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e9/e97c57a3c6375297eb89.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/02/028d86869399db5d0e00.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/68/68f2b7898b12889a14e4.png\" /></div>"},{"n":19,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A search algorithm is defined as follows:<br />\n• First, it performs a Linear Search on the first k elements of a sorted array of size n.<br />\n• If the key is not found in the first k elements, it performs a Binary Search on the remaining (<em>n</em> – <em>k</em>) elements.<br />\nAssume that the key is present uniformly at random in the array.<br />\nIf all operations (comparison, jump, etc.) take unit time, what is the expected number of comparisons<br />\nrequired to search for the key using this algorithm?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/8e/8e7093791d8fc66b4010.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bc/bc3460bb8c3de372b1c1.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b3/b34e38d39ad6853095e9.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/da/da15c6f8c7d562bb3d1f.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a9/a9d4a2beb4b76f76d30c.png\" /></div>"},{"n":20,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>What is the time complexity of following function fun( )? Assume that log(x) returns log value in base 2.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c6/c67f2a8ed8099730cff6.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>T</em>(<em>n</em>)"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>T</em>(<em>n</em> log logn)"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>T</em>(<em>n</em> logn)"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>T</em>(<em>n</em><sup>2</sup>)"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/06/06ac601dfba714e24c93.png\" /></div>"},{"n":21,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following statements about the User Datagram Protocol (UDP) is TRUE?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"UDP guarantees packet delivery using acknowledgments and retransmissions."},{"key":"B","html":"UDP includes a checksum field that covers only the UDP header and data, but not the IP header."},{"key":"C","html":"UDP uses a pseudo-header in checksum computation to verify that data has been delivered in order."},{"key":"D","html":"UDP ensures connection setup before data transmission to avoid loss."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n&bull; UDP is a connectionless, unreliable transport-layer protocol that does not guarantee delivery, order, or retransmission.<br />\n&bull; It uses a 16-bit checksum to detect data corruption.<br />\n&bull; The checksum calculation includes a pseudo-header (from the IP header: source IP, destination<br />\nIP, protocol, and UDP length), but the checksum itself does not protect the IP header.</p>"},{"n":22,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider a reduced 7-bit IEEE floating-point format, with 3 bits for the exponent and 3 bits for the significant (Mantissa). What will be the value which get stored in the system corresponding to (–0.5625)?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1010001"},{"key":"B","html":"0101001"},{"key":"C","html":"1010101"},{"key":"D","html":"1001000"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ea/eaa0e38e5d94bd54bba5.png\" /></div>"},{"n":23,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>A balanced search tree of order 6 allows each internal node to have at most 6 children, and every non-root node must have at least <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/1d/1d64b545d63211ca8ce2.png\" /> children. The height of the tree is defined as follows: a tree with one level has height 0, a tree with two levels has height 1, and so on.<br />\nIf the tree has a height of 4, what is the minimum number of distinct keys that can be stored in it?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[161,161],"answerText":"161","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>161</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/69/69c58ed53f85b5da1ad8.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":24,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The TCP sequence number field is 32 bits long. Which of the following statements about sequence number wrap-around is TRUE?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"TCP sequence numbers wrap around after every 232 segments, regardless of segment size."},{"key":"B","html":"TCP sequence numbers wrap around after transmitting 2<sup>32</sup> bytes of data, which can cause ambiguity if old data is still in the network."},{"key":"C","html":"TCP sequence numbers reset to zero whenever an ACK is lost, causing a wrap-around."},{"key":"D","html":"Sequence number wrap-around cannot occur in TCP because the numbers are regenerated at every connection setup."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":25,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider a preemptive SJF scheduling technique followed by three processes P1, P2, P3. All of these 3 processes arrive at time t = 0 and their total execution time is 10, 30, 20 units respectively. Each process spends first 10% of execution doing I/O, 80% CPU and rest doing I/O operation. What will be the CPU idle % time to execute all processes? (Assume a uniprocessor and all I/O operations can be overlapped)</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"10.642"},{"key":"B","html":"7.692"},{"key":"C","html":"12.469"},{"key":"D","html":"CPU will not be idle"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/12/12b620d00b1ff8f86296.png\" /></div>"},{"n":26,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>A program has 40% load/store instructions. The ideal CPI without memory stalls is 2.2. The instruction cache miss rate is 2%, and the miss penalty is 100 cycles. The program achieves a speedup of 3 with a perfect cache. Determine the data cache miss rate _________? (in % )</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[6,6],"answerText":"6","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>6</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/09/094ddcd721c6628c643d.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9b/9b4bb5db4d7313bb50a0.png\" /></div>"},{"n":27,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the intermediate code given below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d7/d74ee3cd674e832dd0b0.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following code has above intermediate code?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/0e/0ec4d59f24e1e7b03989.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/88/88d8864b0c7e6f5a5b41.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/71/71a25468f841ea855eb2.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c4/c4814e8059f0986a9ed1.png\" /></div>"},{"n":28,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the combinational circuit shown below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/93/9316664a4a0aa56bf2fa.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>If output <em>F</em> = 1, the possible input combinations (<em>I</em><sub>3</sub> <em>I</em><sub>2</sub> <em>I</em><sub>1</sub> <em>I</em><sub>0</sub>) is/are</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0000, 0101"},{"key":"B","html":"0101, 1110, 1010"},{"key":"C","html":"0000, 0010"},{"key":"D","html":"1111, 1000"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nSince, given output of EX-OR gate, <em>F</em> = 1 and one of the input of gate is 1, hence the other input must be zero (0).<br />\ni.e., output of 4 &times; 1 multiplexer is &lsquo;0&rsquo;.<br />\nSince select lines S<sub>1</sub> S0 = 01, hence <em>I</em><sub>1</sub> is propagated to the output. Thus, <em>I</em><sub>1</sub> = 0 in the given possible<br />\ncombinations. Hence, option (a) is correct.</p>"},{"n":29,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following schedule S on operations of three transactions <em>T</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>T</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>T</em><sub>3</sub> in a relational database system:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b3/b39387459cdf879211e3.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Where <em>R<sub>i</sub></em>(<em>X</em>) means transaction <em>T<sub>i</sub></em> read data <em>X</em>, and <em>W<sub>i</sub></em>(<em>X</em>) means transaction <em>T<sub>i</sub></em> write data <em>X</em>.<br />\nConsider the following statements for schedule S:<br />\nS<sub>1</sub> : S is conflict serializable.<br />\nS<sub>2</sub> : 3 serial schedules are conflict equivalent to S.<br />\nWhich of the following is correct?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is correct"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is correct"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> are correct"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is correct"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d4/d456aff1abd6d5f406d5.png\" /></div>"},{"n":30,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the simple graph <em>G</em> given in figure given below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/de/de2a0b903d6f2b3575f7.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Then, which of the following statement(s) is/are correct?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Diameter of the graph is 3."},{"key":"B","html":"(A, F, E, C, D, B, A) is the  Hamiltonian cycle present in the graph."},{"key":"C","html":"Graph is 3-colorable."},{"key":"D","html":"Graph doesn’t have any Eulerian cycle."}],"answer":["A","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c, d)<br />\n1. Recall that the diameter is the maximum of all shortest path lengths between pairs of vertices.<br />\nNote that the shortest path length between D and F is 3, and all other pairs of non-adjacent<br />\nvertices share a neighbor.<br />\n2. One possible solution is (A, F, E, C, D, B, A). This cycle and its reverse should constitute all<br />\npossible solutions.<br />\n3. One possible 3-coloring is: {A, D, E} red; {B, F} green; C blue. Because there exists an oddlength<br />\ncycle (e.g. (B, D, C)), no 2-coloring exists. Therefore the given coloring uses the least<br />\npossible number of colors.<br />\n4. True. This follows from the fact that there exist vertices with odd degree; e.g. B.</p>"},{"n":31,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following languages:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8d/8df7b3cd5fb2fdd49088.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>The number of the above languages which is CFL’s is/are _______.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[2,2],"answerText":"2","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>2</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6d/6d0061be897af6b9ea44.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":32,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following statements correctly describes the operation of CSMA/CA in wireless networks?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"CSMA/CA detects collisions using the carrier sense mechanism, similar to Ethernet (CSMA/CD)."},{"key":"B","html":"CSMA/CA avoids collisions by using acknowledgment frames (ACK) and optional RTS/CTScontrol messages before transmission."},{"key":"C","html":"CSMA/CA relies solely on random backoff timers, without using any acknowledgment mechanisms."},{"key":"D","html":"CSMA/CA allows simultaneous transmissions by using different frequency channels for each node."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":33,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A disk with 200 tracks receives requests at tracks 120, 50, 180, 30, 90, 160, 10 and 70. The initial head position is track 100. Compare the total head movement for the First-Come, First-Served (FCFS) algorithm and the Circular SCAN (C-SCAN) algorithm (assume C-SCAN moves toward higher tracks first and jumps to track 0 after reaching the end).<br />\nWhich of the following is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Average seek time of FCFS = 88.75 tracks"},{"key":"B","html":"Average seek time of C-SCAN = 38.5 tracks"},{"key":"C","html":"Average seek time of C-SCAN = 48.5 tracks"},{"key":"D","html":"Average seek time of FCFS = 78.75 tracks"}],"answer":["A","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5c/5c46c0e8f6a2b45f4293.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c9/c9d199766e3528e60046.png\" /></div>"},{"n":34,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>During an I/O interrupt, the following events are observed:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : Finish execution of the instruction in progress.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : Push the program counter and status registers onto the stack.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>3</sub> : Load the ISR address into the PC.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>4</sub> : Execute the ISR.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>5</sub> : Pop the program counter and status registers to resume the interrupted process.<br />\nChoose the correct sequence of execution:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> → <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> → <em>S</em><sub>3</sub> → <em>S</em><sub>4</sub> → <em>S</em><sub>5</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> → <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> → <em>S</em><sub>3</sub> → <em>S</em><sub>4</sub> → <em>S</em><sub>5</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> → <em>S</em><sub>3</sub> → <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> → <em>S</em><sub>4</sub> → <em>S</em><sub>5</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> → <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> → <em>S</em><sub>4</sub> → <em>S</em><sub>3</sub> → <em>S</em><sub>5</sub>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":35,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following two statements about a directed, simple graph G = (V, E) with distinct, positive edge weights <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/45/45fdbad39656c1435d65.png\" /><br />\nS<sub>1</sub> : If G is a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG), then for any pair of vertices <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/ac/ac06f61898807bcaeaa2.png\" /> there exists a unique shortest <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/83/83237fc4e1dea3ab10d8.png\" /><br />\nS<sub>2</sub> : Let G’ be the underlying undirected graph obtained by ignoring the direction of edges in G.<br />\nThe Minimum Spanning Tree (MST) of G’ is unique.<br />\nWhich of the following is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is true, but <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is false."},{"key":"B","html":"<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is false, but <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is true."},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> are true."},{"key":"D","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> are false."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":36,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following C program:</p>\n\n<p> </p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/dd/ddb0d8034e5f68de8302.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>What will be the output of the above C program when <em>n</em> = 3 is given as input?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"3 1 2 4 7 7 1 6 7"},{"key":"B","html":"3 1 7 2 4 1 6 7 7"},{"key":"C","html":"3 1 2 4 1 6 7 7 7"},{"key":"D","html":"3 1 7 2 4 7 1 6 7"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ae/aefe0d35f5deecb3f4f0.png\" /></div>"},{"n":37,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Let <em>R</em> be the relational schema with n attributes, <em>R</em>(<em>A</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>A</em><sub>2</sub>, ..... <em>A<sub>n</sub></em>) consider the following statements and identify the true statements?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Maximum number of candidate key possible over relational schema is ‘<em>n</em>’."},{"key":"B","html":"Maximum number of candidate key possible is <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/f4/f4a53c0a564495e3b1fb.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"Maximum number of super key occurs when only <em>A</em><sub>1</sub> is candidate key."},{"key":"D","html":"Maximum number of super key occurs when every single attribute is candidate key."}],"answer":["B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5b/5bab1525854a180a20b4.png\" /></div>"},{"n":38,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider a function intended to insert a new node immediately after the first node of a linked list.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3e/3e9af9fae1cf99d68505.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following describes the class of all linked lists for which this function works correctly?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"All linked lists, including the empty list."},{"key":"B","html":"All non-empty linked lists."},{"key":"C","html":"All linked lists with only two nodes."},{"key":"D","html":"Only linked lists where the head node’s next pointer is not NULL."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":39,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider a modified 8-bit floating point representation in which 1-bit for sign, 3-bit for exponent and 4-bit for significant. What will be representation for decimal value –12?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"11111000"},{"key":"B","html":"11101000"},{"key":"C","html":"11110000"},{"key":"D","html":"11111100"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/37/37b5b6967d7702a379aa.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":40,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following grammar:<br />\n<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/89/8966e1572ee98ecc284f.png\" /><br />\nThe above grammar is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"LR(0)"},{"key":"B","html":"SLR(1)"},{"key":"C","html":"CLR(1)"},{"key":"D","html":"LALR(1)"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6f/6f8186336fba56322450.png\" /></div>"},{"n":41,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the sequential circuit shown below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/39/3909fc7484c974d5cd48.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>where, <em>u</em>(<em>k</em>) : input, <em>x</em>(<em>k</em>) : present state, <em>z</em>(<em>k</em>) : output.<br />\nThe state diagram of the above sequential circuit is</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/a5/a5ba28fa1dbba51606ff.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/37/376704f44852cda658de.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/ff/fffb726dedec4aee5517.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/a6/a6511ea875c734260f16.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b9/b903fe40d8f33396f830.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":42,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The output of executing the following program is ________.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/18/18b1c7bb8bf3f343d20a.png\" /></div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[19,19],"answerText":"19","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>19</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bb/bbe233708f961854ba47.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":43,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/92/922c309d22e33c38a4e4.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/29/29aeb9ae211bc11492e4.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/20/20d053ad46a4e9e6a9b8.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2f/2fa9caaaeffdc071c922.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/69/6991bc3c4f636984a2bc.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/78/78a647d3fa525db6bbc1.png\" /></div>"},{"n":44,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A linear-probing hash table of length 10 uses the hash function <em>h</em>(<em>x</em>) = <em>x</em> mod 10. After inserting six integer keys into an initially empty hash table, the array of keys is</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/60/608d26354035af3cc49e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following choice is insertion sequence resulting in the above hash table? Assume that the length of the hash table does not change during the insertions.</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"46, 42, 34, 52, 23, 33"},{"key":"B","html":"34, 42, 23, 52, 33, 46"},{"key":"C","html":"46, 34, 42, 23, 52, 33"},{"key":"D","html":"42, 46, 33, 23, 34, 52"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1b/1b866ba86441e8274ce6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":45,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>During a TCP session setup, Host A advertizes a window scaling factor of 7, and Host B advertizes a window scaling factor of 5. Later in the session, Host A sends data and advertizes a TCP window value of 20,000 bytes in its TCP header. Host B acknowledges and advertizes a TCP<br />\nwindow value of 40,000 bytes. Assuming no congestion or packet loss, determine the actual maximum amount of unacknowledged data that each host can transmit at a time.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Host A → 1,280,000 bytes; Host B → 1,280,000 bytes"},{"key":"B","html":"Host A → 2,560,000 bytes; Host B → 1,280,000 bytes"},{"key":"C","html":"Host A → 1,280,000 bytes; B → 40,000 bytes"},{"key":"D","html":"Host A → 2,560,000 bytes; B → 40,000 bytes"}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n(a) Host A &rarr; 200000 &times; 27 = 2560000 bytes<br />\n(b) Host B &rarr; 40000 &times; 25 = 1280000 bytes<br />\nTherefore, Host A &rarr; 2560000 bytes<br />\nHost B &rarr; 1280000 bytes</p>"},{"n":46,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider two processes A and B accessing the shared variables X and Y, protected by two binary semaphores P and Q, both initialized to 1. Wait and Signal denote the usual semaphore operations: Wait decrements the semaphore value (and waits if 0), Signal increments the semaphore value. The pseudocode of the processes is as follows:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/65/65c56e828c040077d0a9.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following statements is correct?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Mutual exclusion is satisfied, but progress is not guaranteed."},{"key":"B","html":"Progress and mutual exclusion are satisfied, but bounded waiting may be violated."},{"key":"C","html":"Mutual exclusion not satisfied."},{"key":"D","html":"Mutual exclusion, bounded waiting and progression is satisfied."}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":47,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The number of possible finite automata with two states a<sub>0</sub> and a<sub>1</sub> (where a<sub>0</sub> is the initial state over the alphabet {0, 1}) which accepts empty language is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[320,320],"answerText":"320","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>320</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c7/c704cf706ab553c8feda.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":48,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A directed graph G(V, E) with |V| = 6 and |E| = 9 contains a few negative-weight edges but no negative-weight cycles.<br />\nThe graph is represented using an adjacency list (V<sub>1</sub> → V<sub>2</sub>, wt). A variant of the Bellman-Ford algorithm is run from a source vertex S = 0.<br />\nAfter 5 full iterations of edge relaxations, the distance array dist[ ] contains final shortest path distances from S to all other nodes.<br />\nSuppose the edge list is as follows:<br />\n(0 → 1, 4), (0 → 2, 5),<br />\n(1 → 2, –3), (2 → 3, 2),<br />\n(3 → 1, 1), (1 → 4, 6),<br />\n(2 → 5, 4), (5 → 3, –2),<br />\n(4 → 5, –1)<br />\nBased on this, which of the following statements are necessarily TRUE?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Bellman-Ford will update the distance to at least one vertex in every iteration, including the 5<sup>th</sup>."},{"key":"B","html":"The presence of the cycle (1 → 2 → 3 → 1) with total weight 0 implies that Bellman-Ford may keep updating distances indefinitely."},{"key":"C","html":"The final distances will be accurate after at most 5 iterations."},{"key":"D","html":"If we add an edge (3 → 0, –10), Bellman-Ford will detect a negative-weight cycle in the 6<sup>th</sup> iteration."}],"answer":["C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c, d)<br />\n&bull; Bellman-Ford guarantees convergence in |V| &ndash; 1 = 5 iterations only if no shorter paths exist.<br />\nIf no update is made in the final iteration, the algorithm halts early. So, update is not necessarily<br />\nmade in the 5<sup>th</sup>.<br />\n&bull; A cycle with total weight 0 does not cause infinite updates. Only negative-weight cycles do.<br />\nHence, this does not make Bellman-Ford loop indefinitely.<br />\n&bull; Bellman-Ford always converges in at most |V| &ndash; 1 = 5 iterations if there is no negative cycle,<br />\nwhich is stated in the question.<br />\n&bull; Adding an edge (3 &rarr; 0, &ndash;10) creates a cycle: 0 &rarr; 1 &rarr; 2 &rarr; 3 &rarr; 0<br />\nTotal weight: 4 &ndash; 3 + 2 &ndash; 10 = &ndash;7 &rArr; a negative-weight cycle, and Bellman-Ford will detect it in<br />\nthe 6<sup>th</sup> iteration.</p>"},{"n":49,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following program:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/22/223a71ece0e795c2d5c9.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>What is the output of the above program?</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[8,8],"answerText":"8","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>8</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/27/27c4737557e07683eb20.png\" /></div>"},{"n":50,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider a relation <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/9a/9a9ddaaa750cf5d7bcf2.png\" /> defined as:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2a/2a375dce18d6e07b2e5f.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following statements about <em>R</em> is true?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Reflexive, Symmetric, Transitive"},{"key":"B","html":"Symmetric, Not Reflexive, Not Transitive"},{"key":"C","html":"Not Reflexive, Not Symmetric, Not Transitive"},{"key":"D","html":"Symmetric, Not Reflexive, Transitive"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/13/1327026dd508b74ae0de.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":51,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following is incorrect? (/ is right quotient)</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"If <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> are CFL, then <em>L</em><sub>1</sub>/<em>L</em><sub>2</sub> is regular."},{"key":"B","html":"If <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> are regular, then <em>L</em><sub>1</sub>/<em>L</em><sub>2</sub> is regular."},{"key":"C","html":"If <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> are RE, then <em>L</em><sub>1</sub>/<em>L</em><sub>2</sub> is RE."},{"key":"D","html":"If <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> is CFL and <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> is regular then <em>L</em><sub>1</sub>/<em>L</em><sub>2</sub> is CFL."}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nRegular, CFL, and recursive languages are closed under right quotient.</p>"},{"n":52,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following resource allocation graph:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f1/f1c603a97469bae43b5d.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following is true?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The above allocation system is unsafe."},{"key":"B","html":"The system is safe and safety order is <em>P</em><sub>2</sub>, <em>P</em><sub>4</sub>, <em>P</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>P</em><sub>3</sub>."},{"key":"C","html":"The system is safe and safety order is <em>P</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>P</em><sub>2</sub>, <em>P</em><sub>3</sub> and <em>P</em><sub>4</sub>."},{"key":"D","html":"The system is safe and the safety order is <em>P</em><sub>3</sub>, <em>P</em><sub>2</sub>, <em>P</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>P</em><sub>4</sub>."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/eb/eb990b683b0a55b4f456.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":53,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the code given below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d7/d7ba72645f9533e66a18.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following is TRUE?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The output of the above program is 16 21"},{"key":"B","html":"The output of the above program is 15 20"},{"key":"C","html":"The output of the above program 15 32"},{"key":"D","html":"The output of the above program is 21 30"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nInitially, I is equal to zero. I become 5 since case 0 is true, and 16 because there is no break<br />\nstatement until the last statement of the switch block. Because of i++, I becomes 17 before the next iteration begins.<br />\nBecause no case is true in the next iteration, execution returns to default and I becomes 21.<br />\nIn C, if one of the cases is true, the switch block is processed until it reaches the break statement.<br />\nIf there is no break statement, all cases are processed after the true case.</p>"},{"n":54,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statement with respect to relational algebra and select the correct option(s) given below:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The intersection ‘∩’ operator can be derived using ‘–’ minus which is a basic binary operator in relational algebra."},{"key":"B","html":"The join <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/b1/b1d738f05c07b0673da8.png\" /> operator can be derived by using [× (cross product σ (selection and π (projection)] operator."},{"key":"C","html":"The ‘+’ or ‘/’ division operator can be derived by using [× (cross product), σ (selection) and π (projection)] operator."},{"key":"D","html":"The ‘+’ or ‘/’ division operator can be derived by using [× (cross product), π (projection) and ‘–’ minus] operator."}],"answer":["A","B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f0/f02ce3092b039ccc2f41.png\" /></div>"},{"n":55,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the state table of a digital circuit given below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ee/ee3c96e08e70635f9ffa.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>The total number of redundant states present is equal to __________.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[3,3],"answerText":"3","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>3 [3 - 3]</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f4/f4a513239e39ff4fbc11.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":56,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Given a positive integer n, let G = (V, E) be a graph where V = {0, 1}<sup>n</sup>, i.e. V is the set of vertices which has one-to-one correspondence with the set of all n bit binary strings and <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/9e/9e7974ccf9c6fc559201.png\" /> belongs to V}, <em>u</em> and v differ in exactly one bit position?<br />\nFind the size of E if <em>n</em> = 8 ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[1024,1024],"answerText":"1024","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>1024</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f0/f0a7c307eeccd0114d7f.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":57,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In a distance vector routing network, router A has a direct link to B initially, A’s routing table shows:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/51/51e9b547833e71c0fd50.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Now, the link between B and C fails, and no loop prevention (split horizon and poison reverse)<br />\nWhich of the following best describes what happens next?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"A immediately removes C from its table after detecting the failure."},{"key":"B","html":"B marks C as unreachable and stops advertising it."},{"key":"C","html":"A continues to advertise a path to C (via B), and B re-learns a false."},{"key":"D","html":"The network instantly reconverges because of triggered updates."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>"},{"n":58,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following is true about lexical analysis?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"It scans the source code and generates a parse tree."},{"key":"B","html":"It checks the grammar rules and constructs a parse tree."},{"key":"C","html":"It breaks the source code into tokens and generates a token stream."},{"key":"D","html":"It analyzes the meaning of the source code and generates an intermediate code."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nLexical analysis is the first phase of a compiler that breaks the source code into stream of tokens<br />\n(also known as lexemes) that represent the smallest meaningful units of the language syntax.</p>"},{"n":59,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A message is made up entirely of characters from the set P = {<em>W</em>, <em>X</em>, <em>Y</em>, <em>Z</em>}. The table of probability for each characters given below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3d/3d14a8ea782b339abd4b.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following are correct?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"If a message of 200 characters over P are encoded using Huffman encoding then the expected length of the encoded message is 340 bits."},{"key":"B","html":"If a message of 200 characters over P are encoded using Huffman encoding then the expected length of the encoded message is 392 bits."},{"key":"C","html":"<em>W</em> and <em>X</em> having the same number of encoding bits when encoded using Huffman coding."},{"key":"D","html":"<em>X</em> and <em>Y</em> having the same number of encoding bits when encoded using Huffman coding."}],"answer":["B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/59/59421158c715b06307ff.png\" /></div>"},{"n":60,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the below are undecidable?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"{<m> | <em>M</em> is a Turing Machine and <em>M</em> accepts ∈}.</m>"},{"key":"B","html":"{<<em>G</em>> | <em>G</em> is a CFG and <em>G</em> is unambiguous}."},{"key":"C","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/e2/e2aaced7f6f568f498c7.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"Given a CFG G, find whether <em>L</em>(<em>G</em>) = <em>R</em>, where <em>R</em> is regular set."}],"answer":["A","B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, d)<br />\n&bull; (a) is undecidable as this is non-trivial question on RE languages.<br />\n&bull; (b) is ambiguous problem, well known undecidable.<br />\n&bull; (c) is membership problem in CFG which is decidable.<br />\n&bull; (d) is undecidable</p>"},{"n":61,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/99/9965a0a292ffc26202e3.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f0/f0de42fe97e8d129af91.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f2/f2577f7b89e99c73fde3.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/41/41394ad05f343384deff.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4e/4e737e393799d0fb7fb0.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a7/a78c944ef7663d7a7c88.png\" /></div>"},{"n":62,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following statements correctly describe a Passive Open in TCP?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"It is initiated by the server side."},{"key":"B","html":"It sends a SYN segment immediately after opening."},{"key":"C","html":"The TCP state becomes LISTEN."},{"key":"D","html":"It waits for an incoming SYN request."}],"answer":["A","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c, d)<br />\nServer side will be in passive state and it will be in Listen State .<br />\nIt waits for sync segment from client side</p>"},{"n":63,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a Depth First Search (DFS) traversal of a directed graph. Given the set of edges {E} is partitioned into Tree, Back, Forward, and Cross edges, and where Xs and Xe represent the discovery and finish times, respectively.<br />\nWhich of the following statements are correct? (Select all that apply)</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The condition Us < Vs < Ve < Ue must classify the edge (u, v) as a Tree Edge."},{"key":"B","html":"If the graph is a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG), no edge in the set E can satisfy the temporal ordering Vs < Us < Ue < Ve."},{"key":"C","html":"An edge (<em>u</em>, <em>v</em>) satisfying Vs < Ve < Us < Ue} is Cross Edge"},{"key":"D","html":"Both Forward Edges and Cross Edges are non-Tree edges, but only Forward Edges connect a node to one of its descendants in the DFS forest."}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, c, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/89/89e0bb1a02e14236b34c.png\" /></div>"},{"n":64,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following SQL statements:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1d/1d027d1cb68d51f55529.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following SQL statements are valid queries?<br />\n(i) SELECT DEPT, COMMISSION FROM EMP WHERE SALARY > 7000 GROUP BY DEPT<br />\nHAVING COMMISSION > 200;<br />\n(ii) SELECT EMPNAME, SALARY FROM EMP WHERE COMMISSION = NULL AND DEPT =<br />\n‘IT’;<br />\n(iii) SELECT * FROM EMP WHERE SALARY BETWEEN 6000 AND 8000 GROUP BY DEPT ORDER<br />\nBY EMPID;<br />\n(iv)SELECT EMPNAME, DEPT FROM EMP WHERE COMMISSION IS NOT NULL AND SALARY<br />\n> 6000;</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"(ii) and (iii)"},{"key":"B","html":"(iv) and (iii)"},{"key":"C","html":"(i), (iii) and (iv)"},{"key":"D","html":"Only (iv)"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/20/20226378ea9643c70b4d.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":65,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following grammar and semantic rules: A → X Y Z<br />\nX.val = f(A.in)<br />\nY.val = g(X.val)<br />\nZ.val = h(Y.val)<br />\nA.out = k(Z.val) and A → M N<br />\nM.in = N.out Which of the following statements is TRUE?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Both productions are S-attributed"},{"key":"B","html":"Both productions are L-attributed but not S-attributed"},{"key":"C","html":"First production is L-attributed, second violates the L-attributed rule"},{"key":"D","html":"First production is S-attributed, second is L-attributed"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n&bull; In the first production, each attribute depends only on the parent or left siblings &mdash; this is<br />\nL-attributed.<br />\n&bull; In the second, M.in = N.out depends on the right sibling (N), violating L-attributed definition.</p>"}],"math":false,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"General Aptitude","start":0,"count":10},{"name":"Technical","start":10,"count":55}],"stats":{"questions":65,"maxMarks":100,"types":{"MCQ":43,"NAT":11,"MSQ":11},"withoutKey":0}};
